      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the effectiveness of intramuscular diclofenac in postoperative cesarean section pain control."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A randomized controlled trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, King Chulalongkorn Memorial Hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Eighty patients scheduled for elective cesarean section between October 2007 and April 2008."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHOD",
          "content": "All patients had cesarean section performed under spinal anesthesia with spinal morphine and randomized into two groups by a table of randomization. They were to receive diclofenac 75 mg intramuscular every 12 hours for 2 doses or standard rescue drugs (Tramadol)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "The number of patients who required rescue drugs, pain score (VAS), side effects of diclofenac, and satisfaction were evaluated for 48 hours postoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In the diclofenac group, no patient required rescue drug compared to 20% of patients in the control group (p < 0.05). Median pain scores were less in the diclofenac group at 6 hours (1 (range 0-6) vs. 4 (range 0-6), p = 0.002), 12 hours (2 (range 0-5) vs. 3 (range 0-7), p = 0.031), and 24 hours (1.5 (range 0-4) vs. 3 (range 1-8), p < 0.0001), respectively. No side effects of diclofenac (e.g., gastrointestinal bleeding, bleeding tendency, uterine atony, or injection site irritation) were observed Satisfaction was comparable in both groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Diclofenac can be used safely to reduce the requirement of rescue drugs for pain control in postoperative cesarean section."
        }
      ]},
